1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,265
Why are there no bridges over the

2
00:00:02,265 --> 00:00:03,157
Amazon River?

3
00:00:03,507 --> 00:00:06,374
The Amazon flows about six thousand four

4
00:00:06,374 --> 00:00:09,886
hundred kilometers across South America, from the

5
00:00:09,886 --> 00:00:12,179
Andes in Peru to the Atlantic in

6
00:00:12,179 --> 00:00:12,681
Brazil.

7
00:00:13,031 --> 00:00:15,372
It carries more water than any other

8
00:00:15,372 --> 00:00:17,518
river on Earth, around a fifth of

9
00:00:17,518 --> 00:00:19,795
all the river water that flows into

10
00:00:19,795 --> 00:00:21,031
the world's oceans.

11
00:00:21,381 --> 00:00:23,747
And yet, not a single bridge crosses

12
00:00:23,747 --> 00:00:25,455
its main channel. Not one.

13
00:00:25,805 --> 00:00:28,361
Reason one: there aren't many roads to

14
00:00:28,361 --> 00:00:30,983
connect. Most of the river runs through

15
00:00:30,983 --> 00:00:33,807
thick rainforest, and the big cities along

16
00:00:33,807 --> 00:00:36,363
it, like Manaus, are mostly reached by

17
00:00:36,363 --> 00:00:37,304
boat or plane.

18
00:00:37,654 --> 00:00:40,442
Reason two: the river keeps changing. In

19
00:00:40,442 --> 00:00:42,951
the rainy season, the water can rise

20
00:00:42,951 --> 00:00:45,321
more than ten meters and flood the

21
00:00:45,321 --> 00:00:47,830
forest for kilometers on both sides.

22
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:50,542
Reason three: the banks are soft and

23
00:00:50,542 --> 00:00:53,757
keep shifting. Building giant foundations in that

24
00:00:53,757 --> 00:00:56,841
mud would be extremely difficult and expensive.

25
00:00:57,191 --> 00:00:59,805
So people do what they've always done:

26
00:00:59,805 --> 00:01:02,625
they use boats. Ferries carry cars, cargo

27
00:01:02,625 --> 00:01:05,101
and passengers up and down the river

28
00:01:05,101 --> 00:01:05,789
every day.

29
00:01:06,139 --> 00:01:08,610
At Manaus, the dark Rio Negro meets

30
00:01:08,610 --> 00:01:11,294
the sandy Solimões, and the two colors

31
00:01:11,294 --> 00:01:14,118
flow side by side for kilometers without

32
00:01:14,118 --> 00:01:16,802
mixing. It's called the Meeting of the

33
00:01:16,802 --> 00:01:17,296
Waters.

34
00:01:17,646 --> 00:01:20,724
There is one big bridge nearby, over

35
00:01:20,724 --> 00:01:23,631
the Rio Negro at Manaus, opened in

36
00:01:23,631 --> 00:01:26,795
2011. But it crosses a tributary, not

37
00:01:26,795 --> 00:01:28,334
the Amazon itself.

38
00:01:28,684 --> 00:01:30,942
Near the mouth, the Amazon is so

39
00:01:30,942 --> 00:01:33,271
wide that you often can't see the

40
00:01:33,271 --> 00:01:35,812
other side, and the island of Marajó

41
00:01:35,812 --> 00:01:37,929
in its delta is about the size

42
00:01:37,929 --> 00:01:38,988
of Switzerland.

43
00:01:39,338 --> 00:01:41,749
Would you rather cross the Amazon by

44
00:01:41,749 --> 00:01:43,825
boat or by a giant bridge? Tell

45
00:01:43,825 --> 00:01:45,098
me in the comments.

